# ============================================================================
# The spreadsheet is NO LONGER the source of truth (2026-08-30).
#
# timeline-data.json is now the master copy and is edited in the app. This importer is a
# one-off migration tool that has already done its job. See docs/data-store.md.
#
# Running it against the real data file is DESTRUCTIVE. The timeline id is derived from the
# workbook's path, so a re-import always matches the timeline already there and replaces it,
# writing back the importer's defaults for everything the workbook has no column for:
# every image name, every category icon and colour, every confidentiality level, and every
# edit made in the app since.
#
# If you ever need it again, point DATA_FILE_PATH at a scratch file first.
# ============================================================================

# Source spreadsheet to import from.
SOURCE_EXCEL_FILE_PATH='/path/to/data.xlsx'

# Destination JSON timeline store the importer writes.
# Re-running the importer REPLACES the timeline it previously wrote for this workbook
# rather than appending another copy — see the warning at the top of this file.
DATA_FILE_PATH='/path/to/timeline-data.json'

# Values already set in your shell take precedence over this file, so you can safely
# redirect a test run:
#   DATA_FILE_PATH=/tmp/scratch.json dotnet run --project src/Pictura.Vita.Excel.Importer
#
# Rows that fail validation stop the import with a report. Pass --skip-invalid to import
# everything else and skip the bad rows.
